# What pacman cannot do for you, said at the moment you would otherwise have to go looking.
#
# Neither of these is something a package should do on its own: one needs root and changes how your
# user session behaves after logout, the other edits your profile.

post_install() {
    cat <<'MSG'

gozellij is installed. Two steps are yours, in this order:

  1. Keep your services running after you log out:

         sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER
         systemctl --user enable --now gozellijd

  2. Make it what your login shell starts:

         gozellij login-setup            # says what it would change, and changes nothing
         gozellij login-setup -install

     Then open a *new* login session to try it - not the one you are in, whose
     environment still says you are inside the multiplexer you are replacing.

  Check either step with:  gozellij doctor

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

gozellij upgraded. The running daemon keeps the old binary until you tell it:

    systemctl --user reload gozellijd    # exec in place; every service keeps its pid

  Not `restart`: that stops the daemon, and stopping is the one case where the
  descriptors it is holding are deliberately let go.

MSG
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'MSG'

gozellij is gone, but your login shell may still try to start it. Take that out with:

    gozellij login-setup -undo -install    # if you still have the binary

  or by hand: remove the block marked ">>> gozellij login setup >>>" from your
  profile. Until then a login prints one line saying gozellij could not start,
  and gives you an ordinary shell - it cannot lock you out.

MSG
}
